// ed-instrdesign — generated from src/ed-instrdesign-*.js by tools/merge.js. Edit the src files, then run: node tools/merge.js
// Instructional Designer. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["ed-instrdesign"] = (QB["ed-instrdesign"] || []).concat([
["Role", "What does an instructional designer do, and how do you approach a new project?", "**Why they ask:** they want to see you think in outcomes and process, not just slides.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"An instructional designer creates learning experiences that actually change what people can do — not just content, but the design behind it. I start every project by understanding the business or performance problem and the audience, because sometimes the answer isn't training at all. If it is, I define measurable objectives, design the learning around them, build and pilot it, and evaluate whether it worked. I partner closely with subject-matter experts for accuracy while owning the learning design myself.\"\n\nTip: Start with the performance problem, not the content.", "B", "E", null, {"t":1,"f":["What if the 'training need' isn't really a training problem?","I say so — if the gap is process, tools, or motivation, training won't fix it, and I propose the right solution instead of building a course that fails."]}],
["ADDIE", "Walk me through the ADDIE model and how you use it.", "**Why they ask:** ADDIE is the most common ID framework; they want to see you apply it, not just name it.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"ADDIE is Analysis, Design, Development, Implementation, Evaluation. In Analysis I define the problem, audience, and objectives. In Design I map the structure, objectives, assessments and strategy — often in a storyboard or design document. Development is where I build the actual course. Implementation is launch and rollout. Evaluation checks whether learning and performance improved. I use it iteratively rather than strictly linear — I'll prototype and get feedback early rather than waiting until the end — and for faster projects I'll lean on a more agile, rapid approach like SAM.\"\n\nTip: Show you apply ADDIE iteratively, and know agile alternatives like SAM.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["How is SAM different from ADDIE?","SAM (Successive Approximation Model) is more iterative and agile — rapid prototyping and repeated review cycles instead of ADDIE's more sequential phases."]}],
["Objectives", "How do you write effective learning objectives?", "**Why they ask:** objectives are the backbone of sound design.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I write objectives that are measurable and action-oriented — observable verbs tied to what the learner will do, at the right cognitive level. I use Bloom's taxonomy to match the verb to the depth: 'list' and 'define' for recall, 'apply' and 'analyze' for higher skills. For example, instead of 'understand safety procedures' I'd write 'given a spill scenario, select the correct response steps in order.' Good objectives then drive the assessment and the content — if I can't assess it, the objective isn't written right.\"\n\nTip: Observable verb, measurable, and it must be assessable.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why avoid words like 'understand' or 'know' in objectives?","They're not observable or measurable — you can't tell if someone 'understands,' so you anchor to what they can actually do."]}],
["Analysis", "How do you conduct a needs or audience analysis?", "**Why they ask:** analysis prevents building the wrong thing.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I gather data from multiple sources — stakeholder interviews, performance data, observing the work, and talking to the actual audience. I'm looking for the real gap between current and desired performance and its cause. For the audience I profile their prior knowledge, context, constraints, and how and where they'll use the skill, because designing for a busy frontline worker on a phone is very different from a desk analyst. This analysis is what keeps me from defaulting to a generic course.\"\n\nTip: Triangulate data and profile the real audience and context.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["What's a performance gap?","The difference between how people are performing now and how they need to perform — training only helps if the gap is caused by a lack of skill or knowledge."]}],
["Collaboration", "How do you work with subject-matter experts?", "**Why they ask:** the SME relationship makes or breaks ID projects.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I respect that the SME owns the content accuracy and I own the learning design — that division keeps the relationship healthy. I come prepared, make meetings efficient, and ask targeted questions to extract what learners actually need rather than everything the SME knows. The common challenge is SMEs wanting to include far too much, so I help them distinguish need-to-know from nice-to-know by tying everything back to the objectives and the learner's job. I manage their time respectfully since it's usually scarce.\"\n\nTip: You own the design, they own accuracy; filter to need-to-know.", "B", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["How do you handle an SME who wants to cram in everything?","Tie each item to an objective and the learner's actual job — if it doesn't support performance, it becomes a reference resource, not course content."]}],
["Tools", "What authoring tools and technologies do you use, and how do you choose?", "**Why they ask:** practical tooling fluency and judgment.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I've worked with tools like Articulate Storyline and Rise, Adobe Captivate, and video and graphics tools, and I deliver through an LMS using SCORM or xAPI for tracking. I choose based on the need, not habit: Rise for responsive, mostly-linear content that must work on phones; Storyline when I need custom interactions and branching. I'm also careful not to let flashy tool features drive the design — the learning objective decides the interaction, and then I pick the tool that serves it.\"\n\nTip: Let the objective pick the tool, not the other way around.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What's the difference between SCORM and xAPI?","SCORM tracks course completion and scores within an LMS; xAPI can track a much wider range of learning experiences, including outside the LMS."]}],
["Evaluation", "How do you evaluate whether training was effective?", "**Why they ask:** proving impact is what elevates ID from order-taker to strategic partner.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I use the Kirkpatrick levels: reaction (did they find it useful), learning (did they gain the knowledge or skill, via assessment), behavior (are they doing it differently on the job), and results (did the business metric move). Most organizations only measure the first one or two; the valuable work is getting to behavior and results by partnering with the business to track real indicators. I build assessment into the design from the start and set up follow-up measures so I can actually show impact, not just completion rates.\"\n\nTip: Push past 'smile sheets' toward behavior and business results.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why isn't a completion rate a measure of effectiveness?","Completion only tells you people finished — not whether they learned anything or changed how they work, which is the actual point."]}],
["Accessibility", "How do you make learning accessible to all learners?", "**Why they ask:** accessibility is often legally required and is simply good design.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I design to standards like WCAG and Section 508 where they apply: captions and transcripts for audio and video, meaningful alt text for images, keyboard navigation, sufficient color contrast, and not relying on color alone to convey meaning. I also apply Universal Design for Learning — offering multiple means of representation and engagement — which helps everyone, not only learners with disabilities. Building accessibility in from the start is far cheaper and better than retrofitting it at the end.\"\n\nTip: Meet WCAG/508 and design with UDL from the start, not as a retrofit.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What is UDL?","Universal Design for Learning — providing multiple ways to access content, engage, and demonstrate learning so the design works for the widest range of learners."]}],
["Theory", "How does adult learning theory shape your design?", "**Why they ask:** grounding in learning science separates strong IDs from slide-makers.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Adults learn best when it's relevant to a real problem, respects their experience, is practical and immediately applicable, and treats them as self-directed — that's andragogy. So I minimize abstract theory up front, use realistic scenarios and practice, connect to what they already know, and make the 'what's in it for me' explicit. I also respect cognitive load — chunking content and avoiding overload — and build in retrieval practice, because people remember what they actively recall, not what they passively watch.\"\n\nTip: Relevant, practical, respects experience; manage cognitive load.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's cognitive load and why does it matter?","It's the mental effort a task demands; overload the working memory and learning drops, so I chunk content and strip out decorative extras."]}],
["Scenario", "A stakeholder wants a full e-learning course built in two weeks with a reluctant SME. How do you handle it?", "**Why they ask:** real projects are constrained; they test prioritization and pushback.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'd clarify the real goal and audience first, then be honest about trade-offs — quality, scope, and timeline can't all be maxed. I'd propose a scoped version focused on the highest-priority objectives, maybe a rapid tool like Rise and a shorter job aid instead of a polished branching course, and I'd make the SME's time efficient by sending focused questions in advance. I'd deliver a solid minimum viable version on time and plan enhancements for a second phase, rather than promising everything and delivering something broken.\"\n\nTip: Scope to priorities, be honest about trade-offs, phase the rest.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["When is a job aid better than a course?","When the task is occasional or reference-based — people often need support at the moment of work, not a course they'll forget."]}],
["Behavioral", "Tell me about a learning project you're proud of, or one that didn't land.", "**Why they ask:** evidence of real work and reflection.\n\n**Answer (example):**\n\"I built a compliance course that was failing — low scores, lots of complaints — because it was dense narrated slides. I redesigned it around realistic decision scenarios where learners practiced the judgment the policy required, cut the seat time in half, and added a quick job aid. Scores and satisfaction both rose and, more importantly, the team could point to fewer real-world errors afterward. It reinforced that practice beats presentation, and that I should measure the right thing from the start.\"\n\nTip: Show a before/after with a real outcome, and what you learned.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["How do you gather feedback before a full launch?","Pilot with a small representative group, watch them use it, and revise — real users surface problems no internal review catches."]}],
["Closing", "Why instructional design, and what makes a great one?", "**Why they ask:** motivation and self-concept of the craft.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I love the intersection of how people learn, creativity, and solving real problems — turning a messy performance gap into an experience that genuinely helps someone do their job better is deeply satisfying. A great ID is curious about the audience, disciplined about objectives and evidence, a strong collaborator with SMEs and stakeholders, and humble enough to let data and user feedback override their own clever ideas. The best measure of my work isn't how polished it looks — it's whether behavior actually changed.\"\n\nTip: Tie motivation to real impact and name the discipline behind the craft.", "B", "E", null, {"t":1,"f":["What's a common mistake new instructional designers make?","Jumping to building content before doing analysis — producing a nice-looking course that solves the wrong problem."]}]
]);

// Instructional Designer — part 2. Format: [topic, question, answer, 'B'|'D', 'E'|'M'|'S'|'X', source, { t: top, f: [follow-up q, a] }]
QB["ed-instrdesign"] = (QB["ed-instrdesign"] || []).concat([
["Process", "When would you use a rapid, iterative model like SAM instead of ADDIE?", "**Why they ask:** choosing the right process shows maturity.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I use a rapid, iterative approach like SAM when the timeline is tight, requirements are fuzzy, or stakeholders need to see something early to react to — which is most real projects. Instead of fully designing before building, I prototype quickly, get feedback, and refine in cycles, so I catch misunderstandings early and avoid building the wrong thing for weeks. ADDIE's phases still inform my thinking, but I apply them iteratively. The model serves the project, not the other way around.\"\n\nTip: Iterative for fuzzy requirements and tight timelines; prototype early.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What's the risk of a strictly linear design process?","You can spend weeks building to a spec that turns out wrong, because stakeholders often don't know what they want until they see a prototype."]}],
["Design", "What goes into a storyboard or design document, and why do you make one?", "**Why they ask:** storyboarding is a core ID deliverable.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A storyboard maps the course before building: for each screen or section, the objective it serves, the on-screen content, narration or text, interactions, media, and assessment. It lets stakeholders and SMEs review and approve the plan cheaply, before I've invested hours in development where changes are expensive. It also keeps the build aligned to objectives and gives developers or me a clear blueprint. Getting agreement at the storyboard stage is far cheaper than reworking a finished course.\"\n\nTip: Storyboard to get cheap approval before expensive development.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Why review at the storyboard stage rather than after building?","Changes to a storyboard take minutes; changes to a built, narrated, interactive course take hours or days — reviewing early saves the budget."]}],
["Strategy", "How do you design scenario-based or branching learning?", "**Why they ask:** scenarios are high-value for skills and decision-making.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I use scenarios when the goal is judgment or decision-making, not just facts — learners practice realistic situations and see consequences, which transfers far better than reading a policy. I design around authentic decisions the learner actually faces on the job, write plausible distractors and meaningful consequences for each choice, and give feedback that teaches rather than just marking right or wrong. Branching adds cost, so I use it where the decision complexity justifies it and keep it focused on the key decisions.\"\n\nTip: Use scenarios for judgment; authentic decisions, meaningful consequences and feedback.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["When is branching not worth the cost?","When the content is straightforward knowledge or the decisions are simple — a linear design or a quick scenario delivers the same learning for far less effort."]}],
["Strategy", "What's your take on microlearning, and when is it appropriate?", "**Why they ask:** microlearning is popular and often misapplied.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Microlearning — short, focused chunks targeting one objective — works well for just-in-time performance support, reinforcement, and spacing content over time to aid retention. It fits busy learners and mobile delivery. But it's not right for complex skills that need depth, practice and integration; chopping those into tiny pieces loses the coherence. So I use it deliberately where the content and context fit, not as a trend, and I make sure the pieces still add up to the real objective rather than being fragments.\"\n\nTip: Great for reinforcement and just-in-time; wrong for complex, integrated skills.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Why isn't everything better as microlearning?","Complex skills need depth, practice and connection between ideas — fragmenting them into micro-bites can destroy the coherence learning requires."]}],
["Strategy", "How and when do you use gamification?", "**Why they ask:** gamification is often used superficially.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I use game elements — challenge, meaningful choices, progress, feedback, and sometimes points or levels — when they genuinely increase engagement and support the learning goal, not as a shiny layer bolted on top. The strongest 'gamification' is really good instructional design: a compelling challenge and immediate feedback. I'm wary of points and badges that reward completion rather than learning, because extrinsic rewards can undercut real motivation. The game mechanic has to serve the objective, or it's just decoration.\"\n\nTip: Use mechanics that serve learning; beware points that reward clicking, not learning.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What's the risk of badges and points?","They can shift focus to collecting rewards rather than learning, and can undercut intrinsic motivation if they're not tied to genuine mastery."]}],
["Strategy", "How do you decide between instructor-led, e-learning, or blended delivery?", "**Why they ask:** modality choice is a key design decision.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I choose based on the objective, audience and context, not preference. Instructor-led or virtual-live suits complex discussion, practice with coaching, and high-touch skills; self-paced e-learning suits consistent knowledge delivery at scale, flexible timing, and tracking; blended combines them — for example, e-learning for foundational knowledge so live time is spent on practice and application. I also weigh reach, budget, how often content changes, and where learners are. The right mix follows from the goal and constraints.\"\n\nTip: Modality follows the objective, audience, scale and constraints.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why put foundational content in e-learning for a blended course?","So scarce live time is spent on discussion, practice and application — the things that actually need an instructor — not on delivering facts."]}],
["Performance", "When is the right solution a job aid or performance support rather than a course?", "**Why they ask:** good IDs don't default to building courses.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"When a task is occasional, reference-based, or changes often, people don't need to memorize it — they need support at the moment of work. A checklist, decision tree, quick-reference guide or embedded help often outperforms a course they'll forget. I assess whether the gap is truly a knowledge-and-skill gap that needs training, or whether a performance-support tool, better process, or job aid solves it faster and cheaper. Not every request should become a course; sometimes the best ID decision is to not build one.\"\n\nTip: Support at the moment of need often beats a course you'll forget.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["Give an example where a job aid beats training.","A rarely used software procedure — a step-by-step guide at the point of use works better than a course people take once and forget."]}],
["Learning science", "How do you apply cognitive load theory and chunking in your designs?", "**Why they ask:** managing load is central to effective design.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Working memory is limited, so I reduce unnecessary load: I chunk content into meaningful groups, remove decorative extras that compete for attention, present related information together rather than split across screens, and build complexity gradually with worked examples before independent practice. I align visuals and narration so they reinforce rather than redundantly compete. Managing load lets the learner spend their mental effort on the actual learning, not on fighting a cluttered design.\"\n\nTip: Chunk, remove extraneous load, scaffold complexity, align visuals and audio.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["What's extraneous cognitive load?","Mental effort caused by poor design — clutter, confusing navigation, decorative media — that steals capacity from the actual learning."]}],
["Multimedia", "How do you design effective video and multimedia for learning?", "**Why they ask:** multimedia is common and easy to do badly.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I follow multimedia-learning principles: keep videos short and segmented, pair relevant visuals with narration rather than reading dense text on screen, cut decorative media that distracts, and use a conversational, human tone. I script to the objective, add retrieval or interaction so learners aren't passive, and always caption for accessibility. I resist producing slick video for its own sake — production value is worthless if the learner tunes out, so engagement and clarity drive the design, not polish.\"\n\nTip: Short, segmented, visuals-plus-narration, interactive, captioned.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Why not put the full narration text on the slide too?","Reading the same words you hear splits attention and increases load — it usually hurts learning rather than helping, outside of accessibility needs."]}],
["Assessment", "How do you write good assessment items that match the objectives?", "**Why they ask:** weak assessment is a common ID failure.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I align every item to an objective at the right cognitive level — if the objective is 'apply,' the item presents a scenario requiring application, not a definition to recall. I write clear stems, plausible distractors based on real misconceptions, and avoid giveaways and trick questions. I focus on measuring the actual skill rather than reading comprehension or test-taking savvy. For higher-level skills I often use scenarios, performance tasks or simulations instead of multiple choice, because you can't measure judgment with a recall question.\"\n\nTip: Align items to the objective's level; plausible distractors, no tricks.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why use scenarios instead of multiple choice for higher skills?","Because judgment and application can't be measured by recalling a fact — the assessment has to require the learner to actually do the thing."]}],
["Evaluation", "How do you run a pilot or usability test before full launch?", "**Why they ask:** piloting catches problems cheaply.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I test with a small group of real, representative learners before full rollout — watching them go through it, not just asking if they liked it. I look for where they get confused, stuck, or bored, whether the navigation and instructions are clear, whether assessments function, and whether they actually learn. Observing reveals problems no internal review catches. Then I revise based on what I see and, if needed, test again. A pilot is cheap insurance against launching something broken to a large audience.\"\n\nTip: Test with real users, observe them, revise before full launch.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Why observe users rather than just survey them?","People can't always articulate what confused them, but watching them struggle shows you exactly where the design fails."]}],
["Stakeholders", "How do you handle scope creep and shifting stakeholder requests?", "**Why they ask:** managing stakeholders is a daily reality.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I prevent a lot of it by nailing down objectives, audience and scope in writing up front, so there's a shared reference. When new requests come mid-project, I don't just refuse or absorb them — I assess impact on timeline, budget and quality, and bring the trade-off back to the stakeholder to decide: we can add this if we extend the deadline, cut something else, or defer it to phase two. Making the trade-offs explicit keeps the project on track and keeps me from silently eating endless changes.\"\n\nTip: Define scope up front; make the cost of new requests explicit.", "D", "M", null, {"t":1,"f":["How do you say no to a stakeholder's pet idea?","I tie every decision back to the objective and audience — if it doesn't serve them, I explain the trade-off and offer it as a phase-two option."]}],
["Project management", "How do you manage timelines and multiple projects?", "**Why they ask:** IDs juggle competing deadlines and dependencies.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I plan backward from deadlines with milestones and build in review-and-revision time, since SME and stakeholder review always takes longer than people expect. I track dependencies — especially SME availability, which is the usual bottleneck — communicate status proactively, and flag risks early rather than at the deadline. Across multiple projects I prioritize by impact and deadline, batch similar work, and protect focus time. I'd rather surface a slipping timeline early with options than surprise a stakeholder at the end.\"\n\nTip: Milestones with review buffers; manage the SME bottleneck; flag risks early.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What's the most common cause of ID projects running late?","SME and stakeholder review and feedback cycles — I plan generous buffers for them and manage that availability actively."]}],
["Tools", "How do you choose and work with an LMS?", "**Why they ask:** delivery and tracking infrastructure matter.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I think about the audience, what needs tracking, the content types, integrations, and admin capacity — not just features. I make sure my courses are built to the right standard (SCORM or xAPI) so completion and scores track reliably, test thoroughly in the actual LMS because things that work in the authoring tool can break on deployment, and design for the learner's real experience finding and launching content. I partner with the LMS admin on enrollment, reporting and troubleshooting. The LMS should make learning accessible and trackable, not be a barrier.\"\n\nTip: Match the LMS to needs; build to standard and test in the real environment.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Why test the course in the actual LMS, not just the authoring tool?","Tracking, completion and display can behave differently once published to the LMS — testing there catches problems before learners hit them."]}],
["Analytics", "How do you use learning analytics and data beyond completion rates?", "**Why they ask:** data maturity distinguishes strategic IDs.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I look past completions to what the data reveals: assessment results by item to find where learning breaks down, time-on-task and drop-off points that flag confusing or boring sections, and, with xAPI, behavior in simulations or on the job. I use it to improve the course iteratively and to inform stakeholders. The richest data connects learning to performance — did the metric the training targeted actually move — which requires partnering with the business to track it. Data should drive design decisions, not just reporting.\"\n\nTip: Use item-level and behavior data to improve design and connect to performance.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["What does a high drop-off point in a course tell you?","That the section is likely confusing, too long, or boring — it flags exactly where to investigate and redesign."]}],
["Application", "How do you design compliance training that people don't hate and actually learn from?", "**Why they ask:** compliance training is notoriously dreadful and a common ID job.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I make it relevant and realistic instead of a wall of policy text — scenario-based decisions showing how the rules apply to the learner's actual job, focused on what they need to do, not every legal detail. I respect their time by cutting to what matters, use real consequences and examples, and let those who know the material test out where allowed. I still meet the legal and tracking requirements, but I treat 'engaging and effective' and 'compliant' as both achievable, not mutually exclusive.\"\n\nTip: Scenario-based and relevant, focused on action, while meeting legal requirements.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["How do you handle the legal team wanting every detail included?","Tie content to what reduces real risk and behavior change, and put reference detail in a job aid rather than forcing learners through all of it."]}],
["Application", "How would you design an effective onboarding or new-hire program?", "**Why they ask:** onboarding is a high-impact, common ID project.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I design onboarding as a structured journey over time — not a day-one firehose — sequencing what a new hire needs when, blending self-paced basics with human connection and on-the-job practice. I define clear outcomes (what they should be able to do by 30, 60, 90 days), build in role-specific and culture elements, and include manager and buddy touchpoints since people matter more than modules. I'd evaluate it on time-to-productivity and retention, not just completion, and iterate based on new-hire feedback.\"\n\nTip: A staged journey with human connection; measure time-to-productivity and retention.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Why not front-load everything into the first day?","A firehose overwhelms and nothing sticks — spacing content to when it's needed improves retention and reduces new-hire stress."]}],
["Application", "How do you design training for soft skills like leadership or communication?", "**Why they ask:** behavioral skills need different design than knowledge.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Soft skills are built through practice and feedback, not information, so I minimize lecture and maximize realistic practice — role-plays, scenarios, simulations — with specific feedback and reflection. I use models and examples to make abstract skills concrete, build in application to the learner's real situations, and reinforce over time rather than one-and-done, since behavior change needs repetition and support. I also involve managers so the new behaviors are expected and reinforced back on the job, where they actually have to stick.\"\n\nTip: Practice, feedback and reinforcement over time — not an information dump.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why involve managers in soft-skills training?","Because behavior change only sticks if it's expected and reinforced on the job — training alone rarely survives contact with the daily environment."]}],
["Motivation", "How do you design to keep learners motivated and engaged?", "**Why they ask:** engagement is necessary for learning to happen.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I make the relevance explicit — why this matters to the learner — grab attention with a real problem or question up front, give appropriate challenge with early wins to build confidence, and ensure satisfaction through meaningful application. That maps to the ARCS model: attention, relevance, confidence, satisfaction. Beyond the model, respecting the learner's time and intelligence is itself motivating. The strongest engagement comes from content that's clearly useful and well-paced, not from entertainment layered on top.\"\n\nTip: Attention, relevance, confidence, satisfaction — relevance does the heavy lifting.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What's the ARCS model?","Keller's motivation model — Attention, Relevance, Confidence, Satisfaction — a practical framework for designing engaging instruction."]}],
["Strategy", "When do you curate existing content versus create new content?", "**Why they ask:** efficient IDs don't build everything from scratch.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I curate when quality existing resources already cover the need — vendor content, internal documents, reputable external material — because building from scratch is expensive and sometimes redundant. I create when the content is specific to the organization, nothing good exists, or the learning needs careful design that off-the-shelf can't provide. Often the answer is a blend: curate the general, create the context-specific, and design the structure and assessment around both. The goal is the learner's outcome at the best use of resources, not maximum original production.\"\n\nTip: Curate the general, create the context-specific; blend for efficiency.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What's the risk of over-relying on curated content?","It may not fit the organization's context or quality bar, and a pile of links isn't a designed learning experience — curation still needs structure and purpose."]}],
["Retention", "How do you design so learning sticks rather than fades?", "**Why they ask:** retention and transfer are the real goals.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I build in retrieval practice — making learners recall and apply, not just re-read — because retrieval is what cements memory. I space and repeat key content over time rather than cramming it once, use realistic application so it transfers to the job, and provide reinforcement and performance support after the formal event. I also connect new content to what learners already know. Learning that's only encountered once and never retrieved or applied predictably fades, so I design against that from the start.\"\n\nTip: Retrieval practice, spacing, application, and post-event reinforcement.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why is retrieval practice so effective?","Actively recalling information strengthens memory far more than passively reviewing it — the effort of retrieval is what builds durable learning."]}],
["Accessibility", "How do you test a course for accessibility?", "**Why they ask:** accessibility compliance often requires active testing.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I check against WCAG criteria: keyboard-only navigation works, a screen reader can read content and controls in a logical order, images have meaningful alt text, media has captions and transcripts, color contrast meets thresholds, and meaning isn't conveyed by color alone. I test with actual assistive technology and accessibility checkers, not just visual inspection, and ideally get feedback from users with disabilities. Building to standards from the start makes this a check rather than a costly retrofit.\"\n\nTip: Test with keyboard, screen reader and checkers against WCAG — not just by looking.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Why isn't a visual check enough for accessibility?","Many barriers — keyboard traps, missing alt text, poor reading order — aren't visible; you have to test with the actual assistive technology."]}],
["Modern", "How do you think about learning experience design (LXD) versus traditional ID?", "**Why they ask:** the field has evolved toward learner-centered, experience thinking.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"LXD brings a stronger user-experience and learner-empathy lens to instructional design — thinking about the whole journey, the learner's context and emotions, usability, and designing experiences rather than just courses. It complements rather than replaces solid ID fundamentals: objectives, assessment and learning science still matter. In practice I combine both — rigorous instructional design plus genuine empathy for the learner's experience and ecosystem, including performance support and the environment, not just the formal learning event.\"\n\nTip: LXD adds a UX and whole-journey lens to solid ID fundamentals.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["Does LXD mean fundamentals like objectives matter less?","No — objectives, assessment and learning science still anchor the work; LXD just adds empathy, usability and whole-experience thinking on top."]}],
["AI", "How are you using AI in instructional design?", "**Why they ask:** AI is rapidly changing L&D workflows.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I use AI as a productivity tool — drafting content, generating scenario variations, first-pass assessment items, outlines, and media — which speeds up production, but I treat its output as a draft to verify, not finished work, because it can be inaccurate or generic. I keep the instructional judgment, objective alignment, accuracy checking with SMEs, and learner empathy firmly human. I also think about AI-enabled learning experiences like adaptive paths. Used well it frees my time for the design thinking that actually matters; used lazily it produces plausible, ineffective content.\"\n\nTip: AI accelerates production; human judgment keeps it accurate and effective.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What's the danger of leaning on AI-generated content?","It can be inaccurate, generic, or misaligned to the objective — without expert review and real instructional design it produces convincing but ineffective learning."]}],
["Global", "How do you design for a global or multilingual audience?", "**Why they ask:** many organizations deliver training across regions.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I design for localization from the start — avoiding idioms, culture-specific examples and images that won't translate, leaving room for text expansion in other languages, and keeping on-screen text separate from media so it's easier to translate. I consider cultural differences in examples, communication norms and even color meaning, and I involve regional stakeholders to validate relevance. True localization is more than translation; it's adapting the experience so it works and resonates for each audience, which I plan for rather than bolt on later.\"\n\nTip: Design for localization up front; adapt, don't just translate.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["What's the difference between translation and localization?","Translation converts the words; localization adapts examples, visuals, formats and cultural references so the experience truly fits each audience."]}],
["Quality", "How do you ensure quality and do QA on a finished course?", "**Why they ask:** QA prevents embarrassing, trust-eroding errors at launch.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I QA systematically against a checklist: content accuracy (verified with the SME), functionality of every interaction and link, navigation, assessment scoring and feedback, tracking in the LMS, accessibility, and display across devices and browsers. I get fresh eyes to review because I'm blind to my own errors, and I test as a real learner would experience it end to end. Catching a broken link or wrong answer-key before launch protects credibility — errors in deployed training erode trust fast.\"\n\nTip: Systematic checklist, fresh eyes, end-to-end testing before launch.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Why get someone else to review your course?","You're blind to your own mistakes and assumptions — a fresh reviewer and a real test learner catch errors and confusion you can't see."]}],
["Application", "How do you convert existing instructor-led training to effective e-learning?", "**Why they ask:** ILT-to-online conversion is a frequent request.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I don't just record the lecture or dump the slides online — that loses the interaction that made the classroom work. I re-examine the objectives, keep what needs to be live or interactive, and redesign the rest for self-paced learning with built-in interaction, practice and checks to replace the instructor's questioning and feedback. I often make it a blend, moving knowledge to e-learning and keeping live time for practice. The conversion is a redesign for a new medium, not a format change.\"\n\nTip: Redesign for the medium with built-in interaction — don't just record the lecture.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["Why not just record the instructor and post it?","Passive video loses the interaction, questioning and practice that made the live session effective — online needs those rebuilt into the design."]}],
["Impact", "How do you connect training to on-the-job behavior change?", "**Why they ask:** transfer to performance is the ultimate measure.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Transfer rarely happens from a course alone, so I design the whole system: realistic practice in training, performance support at the moment of work, manager involvement to set expectations and reinforce, and follow-up reinforcement over time. I identify the specific behaviors the training targets and, with the business, track observable indicators of whether they're happening on the job. If behavior isn't changing, I look at whether the barrier is skill, environment, tools or motivation — because often it's not something more training fixes.\"\n\nTip: Design the whole system — practice, support, managers, reinforcement — to drive transfer.", "D", "S", null, {"t":1,"f":["Why might good training still not change behavior?","Because the barrier may be the environment, tools, incentives or managers — not a lack of skill, and more training won't fix those."]}],
["Stakeholders", "How do you report results and demonstrate your value to leadership?", "**Why they ask:** communicating impact secures resources and credibility.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I report in the language of the business — tying learning to the performance and outcomes leadership cares about, not just completion counts and satisfaction scores. I share what the data shows, what it means, and what I recommend next, keeping it concise and decision-focused. I set expectations up front about what we'll measure and how. Framing L&D as a driver of business results, with evidence, is what moves it from an order-taking function to a strategic partner leadership invests in.\"\n\nTip: Report in business terms with evidence and recommendations, not just activity.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["What's the problem with reporting only completions and satisfaction?","They show activity, not impact — leadership cares whether performance improved, and activity metrics alone keep L&D seen as a cost, not a driver."]}],
["Collaboration", "How do you handle multiple SMEs who give conflicting input?", "**Why they ask:** conflicting expert input is a common snag.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I surface the conflict rather than silently picking one, since the learners can't be taught two contradictory things. I clarify whether it's a genuine disagreement or different contexts, bring the SMEs together to resolve it with a designated decision-maker if needed, and anchor the discussion on what the learner actually needs to do their job. I document the agreed answer. My role isn't to adjudicate the content truth but to get a clear, authoritative answer and keep the project moving.\"\n\nTip: Surface the conflict, get a designated decision-maker, anchor on learner need.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Who should make the final call on conflicting content?","A designated content owner or decision-maker — I facilitate and document, but the authority on accuracy rests with the business, not me."]}],
["Learning science", "How do you scaffold complex skills for learners?", "**Why they ask:** scaffolding is fundamental to skill-building.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I break a complex skill into components, teach and let learners practice each with support, then gradually remove the support as competence grows — worked examples first, then partially completed problems, then independent practice. I sequence from simpler to more complex and from concrete to abstract, and I give feedback at each stage. Scaffolding prevents the overwhelm and failure that come from asking learners to do the whole complex task before they're ready, while still building toward genuine independence.\"\n\nTip: Worked examples to guided practice to independence; remove support gradually.", "D", "M", null, {"f":["What's a worked example and why start there?","A fully solved problem the learner studies first — it shows the method and reduces cognitive load before they attempt it themselves."]}],
["Reflection", "Tell me about a time you got critical feedback on your design. How did you respond?", "**Why they ask:** coachability and iteration.\n\n**Answer (example):**\n\"A pilot group found a course I was proud of confusing to navigate and too long. It stung, but I watched the recordings, saw exactly where they got lost, and realized I'd designed for myself, not them. I simplified the navigation, cut a third of the content to the true essentials, and added clearer wayfinding. The relaunch tested far better. I learned to hold my design loosely and let real learner feedback, not my attachment to my work, drive the final product.\"\n\nTip: Show you let learner feedback override your own preferences.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["How do you separate useful feedback from noise?","Look for patterns across multiple users and tie it to the objective — one person's taste is noise; several learners failing the same way is signal."]}],
["Growth", "How do you stay current in a fast-changing L&D field?", "**Why they ask:** the field and its tools evolve quickly.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I follow L&D communities and thought leaders, learn new tools hands-on, keep up with learning-science research so I'm grounded in evidence rather than fads, and build things to practice. I evaluate trends critically — asking whether something actually improves learning or is just hype — and I grow my portfolio with real projects. The tools change constantly, but the fundamentals of how people learn are durable, so I invest in both: current tooling and timeless learning science.\"\n\nTip: Keep current on tools and trends, grounded in durable learning science.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["How do you tell a genuine advance from hype?","Ask whether evidence shows it improves learning or performance — if it's all novelty and no evidence of outcomes, I treat it skeptically."]}],
["Strategy", "How do you push back when stakeholders ask for training that won't solve their problem?", "**Why they ask:** consultative courage is a mark of a strong ID.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I do a quick analysis of the real performance gap and its cause before agreeing to build. If the issue is a broken process, bad tools, unclear expectations or lack of motivation, training won't fix it and I say so — diplomatically, with evidence — and propose what actually would, whether that's a job aid, a process change, or working with managers. Building a course everyone knows will fail wastes everyone's time and burns my credibility. Being a true partner sometimes means recommending against training.\"\n\nTip: Diagnose the real cause; recommend the right solution even if it isn't a course.", "D", "S", null, {"f":["How do you push back without alienating the stakeholder?","Lead with their goal, show the evidence on the real cause, and offer a solution that actually gets them the outcome they want — I'm helping, not refusing."]}],
["Portfolio", "What should a strong instructional-design portfolio show?", "**Why they ask:** portfolio quality is how IDs are evaluated.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A strong portfolio shows my process and thinking, not just pretty finished screens — the problem, the audience, the objectives, design decisions and why I made them, and ideally the results. I include a range of modalities and a couple of pieces I can speak to in depth. I'd rather show a few strong, well-explained projects than a pile of samples. It demonstrates I solve learning problems, not just operate authoring tools, which is what actually distinguishes a designer.\"\n\nTip: Show process and results, not just polished screens.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Why show your process, not just the final product?","The finished screen doesn't reveal whether you solved the right problem — the process shows your analysis, decisions and judgment, which is the real skill."]}],
["Career", "How do you see the instructional designer evolving into a strategic L&D partner?", "**Why they ask:** vision and ambition for the role.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The order-taker who builds whatever course is requested adds limited value. The strategic partner starts from business performance problems, recommends the right solution — which isn't always training — and measures real impact. Over time I want to move further upstream: consulting on performance, influencing how the organization builds capability, using data to guide decisions, and designing whole learning ecosystems rather than isolated courses. That's where ID creates the most value and where I want to grow, grounded always in solid design craft.\"\n\nTip: From course-builder to performance consultant who measures real impact.", "B", "S", null, {"f":["What skills does that strategic shift require?","Business acumen, consulting and data skills, and the courage to recommend against training — on top of strong design fundamentals."]}],
["Closing", "Why instructional design as a career, and what makes you effective at it?", "**Why they ask:** motivation and self-awareness.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I love sitting at the intersection of how people learn, creativity and solving real problems — turning a messy performance gap into something that genuinely helps people do their work better is deeply satisfying. I'm effective because I'm disciplined about objectives and evidence, genuinely curious about the audience, a strong collaborator with SMEs and stakeholders, and humble enough to let data and user feedback beat my own clever ideas. I measure my success by whether behavior actually changed, not by how polished the course looks.\"\n\nTip: Tie genuine motivation to disciplined, evidence-based, learner-centered practice.", "B", "E", null, {"t":1,"f":["What's the biggest mistake you see in the field?","Building content before doing analysis — producing a polished course that solves the wrong problem, which wastes effort and erodes L&D's credibility."]}]
]);

