QB["ed-instrdesign"] = (QB["ed-instrdesign"] || []).concat([
["Project management", "How do you estimate the time or level of effort for a project?", "**Why they ask:** accurate estimates protect timelines and credibility.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I estimate based on the type and complexity of the deliverable — rough industry ranges exist, like far more development hours per finished hour for highly interactive e-learning than for simple content — adjusted for SME availability, number of review cycles, media complexity and how defined the content is. I build in buffer for the review cycles that always run long, am honest about unknowns, and refine the estimate as scope firms up. I'd rather give a realistic range with assumptions stated than a precise number I can't hit.\"\n\nTip: Estimate by deliverable complexity, state assumptions, buffer review cycles.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Why does interactive e-learning take so much longer per finished hour?","Branching, custom interactions, media and assessment all multiply development time compared to linear content — more interactivity means far more build effort."]}],
["Accessibility", "How do you design for neurodiverse learners and varied cognitive needs?", "**Why they ask:** cognitive accessibility is often overlooked.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I apply Universal Design for Learning: clear, consistent structure and navigation, plain language, content chunked to manage cognitive load, multiple ways to engage with and demonstrate learning, and options like adjustable pacing and reduced distraction. I avoid unnecessary time pressure, decorative clutter and reliance on a single sensory channel. These choices help neurodiverse learners — including those with ADHD, autism or learning differences — and genuinely improve the experience for everyone, so I build them in from the start rather than treating them as a special case.\"\n\nTip: UDL, clear structure, managed load, flexible pacing — helps everyone.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["Why does cognitive accessibility help all learners?","Clear structure, plain language and managed cognitive load reduce confusion and overload for everyone, not just neurodiverse learners — it's simply better design."]}],
["Constraints", "How do you deliver quality learning with little or no budget?", "**Why they ask:** real projects often have severe resource limits.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I focus ruthlessly on the objective and the highest-impact design choices, which often cost little — good scenarios, clear structure, practice and feedback don't require expensive production. I lean on free or existing tools, curate quality existing content instead of building everything, and favor simpler formats like a well-designed job aid or a short interactive over costly custom media. I'm honest with stakeholders about trade-offs. Good instructional design is about the thinking, not the production budget, so constraints push me toward essentials, which is often healthy.\"\n\nTip: Prioritize high-impact, low-cost design; curate; favor simple effective formats.", "B", "M", null, {"f":["What low-cost design choice delivers the most learning?","Realistic practice with good feedback — it drives learning far more than expensive media, and it costs design thinking, not production dollars."]}]
]);
